INT. ST. CASIMIR LANES - CONVERTED CHURCH - NIGHT

A nave of oiled maple lanes where the pews once stood. Stained glass glows behind the pin decks. The league scoreboard hangs where the crucifix used to be.

Four men and women sit at the last lane in the last frame of the season. No one is cheering. The place is nearly empty.

ROSALIND PARKHURST, 60s, cardigan, bowling shoes polished like church shoes, wipes her ball with a towel. Beside her, her son DEACON PARKHURST, 30s, sits with his elbows on his knees, staring at his phone without reading it.

Across the lane sit their opponents. NADINE FOLASADE, 40s, braids tied back, rolls her shoulders. Next to her, an old man in a bowling shirt, GUS TREVELYAN, 70s, tape on two fingers, a cigarette unlit between them.

The pin deck resets with a long mechanical groan.

ROSALIND
Your father used to say the pins knew.

DEACON
Knew what?

ROSALIND
Whether you wanted it.

Deacon puts the phone away.

DEACON
Mom, it's a Tuesday.

ROSALIND
It's the Tuesday.

Nadine gets up. Walks to the ball return. Picks up a green ball. Weighs it. Puts it back. Picks up a blue one.

NADINE
You boys have been on our lane too long.

GUS
Twelve years.

NADINE
Twelve years, and nobody has said a word.

GUS
Because there was nothing to say.

NADINE
There was plenty.

Deacon looks at his mother. Rosalind does not look at him.

DEACON
Is there something I should know?

ROSALIND
Take your turn.

DEACON
I'm asking.

ROSALIND
Take your turn, Deacon.

He stands. Picks his ball. Steps to the line. Pauses.

DEACON (CONT'D)
Dad used to bring us here Sundays.

GUS
Your dad used to bring a lot of people here Sundays.

Deacon turns. Nadine has gone very still. Rosalind has put the towel down.

ROSALIND
Gus.

GUS
Might as well. Twelve years is long enough.

NADINE
Don't.

GUS
Your father had two families, son. This lane was where they met without knowing.

DEACON
(quiet)
Say that again.

GUS
I've been polite twelve years.

NADINE
Gus, shut your mouth.

ROSALIND
He's not lying.

Deacon looks at the ball in his hands. His grip tightens. The leather creaks.

DEACON
Nadine?

NADINE
(evenly)
Your father was my father too.

A long silence. Somewhere in the rafters a pigeon shifts.

Deacon sets the ball down. It rolls, slow, down the lane, crosses the arrows, kisses the head pin, and drops all ten.

Nobody looks at the scoreboard.

ROSALIND
Strike.

NADINE
(not smiling)
Your game.

DEACON
(turning to Gus)
Why tonight?

GUS
Because it's the last frame, son. After tonight there's no lane to share.

Deacon walks away from the line. He passes his mother. He passes Nadine. He stops at the end of the lane, where the stained glass throws red light across his hands.

He picks up the blue ball. Holds it out to Nadine.

DEACON
Roll one.

She hesitates. Then she takes it.
